.TH CCREPO 1 "2026-07-26" "atelier instruments" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccrepo \- Claude Code usage totals, grouped and filtered any way you like
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccrepo
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B ccrepo
.BI \-g " list"
.RI [ filters ]
.RI [ \-\-sort
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-\-top
.IR n ]
.br
.B ccrepo
.RB [ \-\-json | \-\-csv | \-\-flat ]
.RB [ \-\-fx
.IR code ]
.br
.B ccrepo \-\-from\-archive
.RB [ \-\-dest
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-materialise ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ccrepo
totals what Claude Code has cost you and lets you slice that total along any
dimension the logs record \(em by repository, model, git branch, main thread
versus subagent, entrypoint, Claude Code version, agent type, or any calendar
bucket from year down to hour \(em nested however you ask.
.PP
It reads Claude Code's raw session logs under
.I ~/.claude/projects/
directly and computes cost itself,
.BR "per message" ,
from a local price table. Working at the single-message grain is the whole
point: it is what lets
.B ccrepo
group by dimensions that vary
.I within
a session \(em the branch a message was sent from, whether it came from the main
thread or a subagent, which Claude Code version wrote it \(em not just the
coarse repo/model/day totals a per-session tool can offer.
.PP
Those live logs are ephemeral \(em Claude Code deletes them after
.I cleanupPeriodDays
(default 30). With
.B \-\-from\-archive
.B ccrepo
reads the compressed mirror
.BR ccarchive (1)
maintains instead, so totals reach back past that prune horizon. See
.B SOURCE
under OPTIONS.
.PP
Cost here is an API-equivalent
.BR estimate :
what the usage would bill at Anthropic's public list prices, not what your
subscription actually charges. For a plan holder the two differ, and the
.B BILLING
section below explains the optional
.I Actual
column that reconciles them.
.SS How cost is computed
Each message carries five classes of token \(em input, output, cache read, and
a cache write split into 5-minute and 1-hour tiers.
.B ccrepo
prices each class from a built-in per-model table (USD per million input
tokens, keyed by the short model name via longest-prefix match) and the four
standard multipliers Anthropic applies to the other classes. The table is a
dated snapshot; a machine-local
.I ~/.claude/ccrepo-pricing.json
overrides or extends it without touching the tool. See
.B COST AND RECONCILIATION
below.
.SS ccusage as the oracle
.B ccrepo
computes its own cost, but it does not ask you to trust that blindly. On every
run it also invokes
.BR ccusage (1)
and reconciles its own grand total against ccusage's, reporting the drift in
both dollars and percent as a footnote under the table. ccusage is the
.IR oracle :
when the two agree the local price table is current; when they diverge the
footnote says so, per model, so a stale price announces itself instead of
lying quietly. The cross-check is skippable for a faster or offline run \(em see
.BR \-\-no\-reconcile .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Source
.TP
.B \-\-from\-archive
Read the compressed mirror
.BR ccarchive (1)
maintains, rather than the live logs under
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ .
This surfaces sessions Claude Code's cleanup has already pruned, so totals reach
back over the whole preserved history. The archive location resolves exactly as
in
.BR ccarchive (1):
.BR \-\-dest ,
then
.BR $CCARCHIVE_DEST ,
then the iCloud Drive default. In this mode the
.BR ccusage (1)
cross-check is off \(em ccusage reads the live store, which no longer holds the
pruned sessions, so the comparison would be structurally short rather than a
real drift.
.TP
.BI \-\-dest " dir"
Where the archive lives. Implies
.BR \-\-from\-archive .
.TP
.B \-\-materialise
In archive mode, also read files iCloud has evicted to dataless placeholders.
.B ccrepo
reads every file to sum spend, so by default an evicted mirror is skipped and
counted as a stated gap (reading it would fault its bytes back down from
iCloud); this opts into reading \(em and thus re-downloading \(em them.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-rollup
In archive mode, ignore the rollup ledger and re-walk (and re-gunzip) every file
from scratch, writing nothing back. The ledger is a transparent speed cache that
is used automatically when present; this bypasses it \(em for a from-scratch
verification, or to sidestep a ledger you suspect is stale. See
.B ROLLUP LEDGER
below. Outside archive mode it has no effect (there is no cache to bypass).
.SS Grouping
.TP
.BI \-g " list" "\fR, \fP" \-\-group " list"
An ordered, comma-separated list of dimensions, nested left to right. Valid
dimensions are
.IR repo ", " session ", " model ", " branch ", " kind " (main | subagent), "
.IR entrypoint ", " cc\-version ", " agent ", " year ", " month ", " week ", "
.IR day " and " hour .
The default is
.IR repo .
Use
.I total
(or an empty list) for a single grand total with no breakdown. For example
.I \-g repo,model
totals each repo and breaks it down by model;
.I \-g model,repo
inverts that nesting. An unknown dimension is refused.
.PP
Grouping by
.I session
keys on the full UUID but prints an eight-character prefix \(em the same prefix
.B \-\-session
takes, where the whole id would be 36 columns of mostly noise. The
machine-readable outputs keep the full id, because a truncated id you cannot
look up is worse than a wide one you can. Note that several hundred sessions
emit several hundred rows: this dimension is meant to be narrowed with filters,
as in
.IR "\-g session \-\-repo ros \-\-since 20260701" .
.SS Filtering
Each filter takes a comma-separated list. Within one filter a comma means OR; a
leading
.B !
on a term excludes it; a
.B *
globs; matching is case-insensitive. Across different filters the conditions
combine with AND. Most dimensions match exactly, with two conveniences:
.B \-\-model
matches as a substring and
.B \-\-session
matches as a UUID prefix.
.TP
.BI \-\-repo " list"
Restrict to repositories, e.g.
.I \-\-repo ros,faves
or
.IR "\-\-repo '!scanme'" .
.TP
.BI \-\-model " list"
Restrict to models by substring, e.g.
.IR "\-\-model opus" .
.TP
.BI \-\-branch " list"
Restrict to git branches, e.g.
.IR "\-\-branch 'client\-*'" .
.TP
.BI \-\-kind " list"
Restrict to
.I main
or
.I subagent
messages.
.TP
.BI \-\-entrypoint " list"
Restrict to an entrypoint, e.g.
.I cli
or
.IR claude\-vscode .
.TP
.BI \-\-cc\-version " list"
Restrict to a Claude Code version, e.g.
.IR 2.1.209 .
.TP
.BI \-\-agent " list"
Restrict to an agent type.
.TP
.BI \-\-session " list"
Restrict to sessions by UUID prefix, e.g.
.IR "\-\-session 01c3,ff97" .
.TP
.BI \-\-since " YYYYMMDD"
Include only messages on or after this local date.
.TP
.BI \-\-until " YYYYMMDD"
Include only messages on or before this local date.
.TP
.BI \-\-context " range"
Restrict to whichever
.B sessions
had a peak context window in
.IR range \(em
admitting every message of a matching session, never a per-message test. This
is the one filter whose grain is not the message; see
.B CONTEXT SIZE
below.
.I range
is
.IR MIN\-MAX ,
.IR MIN\- ,
or
.IR \-MAX ,
each bound an integer or decimal with an optional
.I k
(\(mu1,000) or
.I m
(\(mu1,000,000) suffix, e.g.
.IR "\-\-context 100k\-500k" ,
.IR "\-\-context 400k\-" ,
or
.IR "\-\-context \-100k" .
A spec with no dash, no bound on either side, or a lower bound above the upper
one is refused with an error, never silently matched as everything.
.TP
.BI \-z " tz" "\fR, \fP" \-\-timezone " tz"
Passed through to
.BR ccusage (1)
for the reconciliation cross-check, so both sides bucket dates in the same zone.
.SS Sorting and row limits
.TP
.BI \-\-sort " list"
A per-level sort, aligned position-for-position to
.BR \-g ;
a single value broadcasts to every level. The comma still separates
.B levels
of
.BR \-g \(em
that positional meaning is unchanged, and is not up for re-reading (decided
2026\-07\-26). Within one level, chain keys with
.B +
to break ties, e.g.
.IR "cost+name" :
sort that level by cost, then alphabetically among equal costs. Each key may
carry its own
.BI : dir
suffix
.RB ( asc " or " desc ),
e.g.
.IR "cost:desc+name:asc" .
Keys are
.IR cost ", " tokens ", " count " (distinct sessions), " name " and " time .
The default is chronological for time dimensions and cost-descending for
everything else. An unknown key or direction is refused. A single key with no
.B +
behaves exactly as before \(em
.I \-\-sort cost
still broadcasts to every level.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " n"
Keep only the first
.I n
rows of
.B each level
after sorting \(em applied recursively, not just at the leaves: under
.IR "\-g repo,model \-\-top 5" ,
the top 5 repos are listed and, within each, the top 5 models. A level's own
subtotal is unaffected \(em it is computed over every event in that group,
never just the rows still listed under it \(em and the grand
.B TOTAL
row always reflects the full, untruncated data; only the enumerated child rows
shrink. Exists because
.I \-g session
alone can emit several hundred rows.
.SS Output format
.TP
.B \-\-flat
Emit one column per group level (a spreadsheet shape) instead of the default
indented tree with subtotals.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit tidy JSON: a
.I meta
block plus one record per leaf, each dimension its own field. Mutually
exclusive with
.BR \-\-csv .
.IP
These records are deliberately
.B wider than the table:
a terminal column is width-bound and a data file is not, so every figure that
was computed ships here even where no column could fit it. Beyond the table's
metrics that means the whole context distribution \(em
.IR contextSessions ,
.IR contextMin ,
.IR contextP25 ,
.IR contextMedian ,
.IR contextP75 ,
.IR contextP90 ,
.I contextMax
and
.I contextMean
\(em where the table shows two of those figures.
.I contextP90
is what distinguishes a lone outlier from a fat tail;
.I contextMean
against
.I contextMedian
is the skew tell. Under a billing config the records also carry
.I coveredTokens
and
.IR uncoveredCost ,
the two inputs the
.I Actual
figure is derived from, so the apportionment can be re-derived rather than
taken on trust.
.IP
The grand total ships as
.I meta.total
rather than as a row, because
.B \-\-json
promises one record per leaf with no subtotals mixed in \(em and because the
whole-set context figures genuinely cannot be re-aggregated from the leaves:
peaks do not recompose once a session is split across groups.
.TP
.B \-\-csv
Emit the same tidy shape as CSV, carrying the same wide field set. Mutually
exclusive with
.BR \-\-json .
.SS Currency
.TP
.BI \-\-fx " code"
Convert all amounts to an ISO-4217 currency, fetched live. The default is
.IR nzd ;
pass
.I usd
for the raw list-price figures with no conversion. A non-USD currency needs
network access to fetch the rate.
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " n"
Force the USD-to-target rate instead of fetching it (useful offline). Has no
effect on USD figures, and must be positive.
.SS Reconciliation and billing
.TP
.B \-\-no\-reconcile
Skip the
.BR ccusage (1)
cross-check. Faster, and works offline, at the cost of leaving the local total
unchecked against the oracle.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-billing
Ignore
.I ~/.claude/ccrepo-billing.json
and report the estimate only, even when a plan config is present.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a concise usage digest and exit. This manual is the full reference.
.SH CONTEXT SIZE
The
.B Context med/max
column answers a different question from the token columns beside it: not how
much a group
.I consumed
but how
.I large the windows got
\(em the signal that a session is running heavy and heading for a compaction.
.PP
Context at one request is everything the model was
.BR sent :
input plus cache-creation plus cache-read tokens. The cached prefix counts \(em
it is in the window however it was billed \(em so reading
.I input_tokens
alone would show a long session as tiny. Output is excluded: it was not in the
window the request was served against. This is the same definition
.BR cctranscript (1)
reports per session, so a peak reads identically in both tools.
.PP
Context is the one metric here that is
.B never summed.
Every message in a session carries that session's whole cached prefix, so adding
message context sizes counts the same window over and over; a "total context"
would be a meaningless multiple of itself. Instead each session contributes its
.BR peak ,
and the column reports two figures over those peaks:
.TP
.B median
What a typical session in the group weighs \(em the habit.
.TP
.B max
The largest window any session in the group reached \(em the alarm.
.PP
Both, because either alone misreads. A group whose sessions sit around 110k but
which once reached 578k looks dangerous on the max and calm on the median; the
pair says "usually fine, once wasn't", which is the actual situation. The mean
is not shown \(em it is dragged by exactly those outliers \(em but it ships in
.BR \-\-json .
.PP
Peaks are per
.IR session ,
not per message, because every session ramps up from near-empty: a message-grain
median understates the weight sessions actually reach. When a session is split
across groups \(em it ran on two branches, say \(em each slice reports the
largest window reached while in that slice, which is what a per-slice reading
means; the parent row still reports the session's true peak.
.PP
There is deliberately
.B no percentage-of-window
figure. The logs record which model served a request but not the size of its
context window, and the 200k and 1M variants of a model log under identical
names \(em so a share-of-window figure could not be computed, only guessed.
The absolute token count is the honest form.
.PP
Subagent logs are folded into their parent session, so a session's peak is the
largest window any of its threads reached, the main thread and its subagents
alike. That answers "did any window get large"; to read the main thread on its
own, filter with
.BR "\-\-kind main" .
.PP
.B \-\-context
filters on exactly this peak, and only at this grain \(em by
.BR session ,
never by message. A message-grain reading would be near-meaningless: every
session ramps up through every band below its eventual peak, so almost every
session would match almost every band almost everywhere. Pairs directly with
.IR "\-g session" :
.I "\-g session \-\-context 500k\-"
answers "which sessions blew past 500k", the question that otherwise needs an
ad-hoc script.
.SH COST AND RECONCILIATION
The built-in price table records, per model, the USD list price per million
.I input
tokens. The other four token classes derive from that base by Anthropic's
standard multipliers \(em output at 5\(mu, cache read at 0.1\(mu, a 5-minute
cache write at 1.25\(mu and a 1-hour cache write at 2\(mu \(em so one base
number prices all five classes. Model names match by longest prefix, so a dated
model id such as
.I haiku\-4\-5\-20251001
resolves to the
.I haiku\-4\-5
base. A model absent from the table is counted at
.B $0
and flagged, never silently guessed.
.PP
A price is effective
.I from
a date
.IR to " a date."
An entry is either a bare number, meaning that price always, or a list of
.RI { from ", " to ", " base }
intervals \(em ISO calendar dates, UTC, both ends inclusive, either end omitted
for open-ended. Each message is priced at the rate in force when it was
.IR sent ,
so a rate change no longer rewrites history: a message from June is priced at
June's rate. A timestamp that falls inside no interval is
.B unpriced
\(em counted at $0 and flagged, exactly as an unknown model is, never snapped to
the nearest interval. The footnote distinguishes the two gaps, because a model
missing from the table needs a price added while a model already in it needs an
interval widened. The shipped table uses this for
.IR sonnet\-5 ,
whose introductory rate runs through 2026-08-31 and reverts the day after: both
figures are entered, each correct on its own side of the date, so the change
needs nobody to remember it.
.PP
The table is a snapshot, checked against ccusage's own per-model cost on real
logs at the time it was taken. Because list prices move, every run reconciles
.BR ccrepo 's
grand total against
.BR ccusage (1)
and prints the drift. The reconciliation footnote reads out mine-versus-ccusage
totals, the delta in dollars and percent over the matched sessions, the largest
per-model divergences, and any sessions seen by only one side (a scope or
pruning difference). A large or growing drift is the signal to refresh the price
table \(em drop an updated
.I ~/.claude/ccrepo-pricing.json
or edit the built-in snapshot. Because ccusage cannot honour
.BR ccrepo 's
non-date filters, the cross-check is computed over the date-comparable scope,
not the fully filtered display set.
.SH BILLING
By default every figure is an API-equivalent estimate at list prices. A
machine-local
.I ~/.claude/ccrepo-billing.json
plan config adds an
.I Actual
column alongside the estimate: tokens on a plan-covered model cost
.B $0
marginal, and the flat monthly plan fee is apportioned across usage \(em by
covered-token share where there is covered usage in range, otherwise by
total-token share \(em plus any uncovered per-token spend. The config names the
plan, its monthly cost, which model families it covers, and any per-token
carve-outs. Absent, or suppressed with
.BR \-\-no\-billing ,
.B ccrepo
reports the estimate only. The plan fee shown is one month; over a multi-month
range the true outlay is (months \(mu fee). The billing model is described in
full in
.I instruments/README.md
in the atelier repository.
.SS Actual spend versus the estimate
The
.I Actual
column reprices usage under the plan, but it does not on its own answer the
money question: over the whole range, is what you
.I genuinely pay
more or less than the API list price would have been? An optional
.I spend
block in the billing config supplies the genuine outlay and
.B ccrepo
reconciles it against its own estimate \(em the money-side analog of the ccusage
cross-check \(em in a footnote that reads out billed versus estimate, the delta
in dollars and percent, and the months it spans. Two modes:
.B plan
(the default) takes billed as the plan fee times the distinct months in scope,
plus any uncovered per-token spend \(em making the
.RI ( months " \(mu " fee )
outlay observable and reconciled;
.B usage
sums an invoiced or metered figure you record per month, so a metered or
tier-changing bill is exact. A month with no recorded figure is reported as a
gap, never smeared into the delta, and a range the config cannot answer for
degrades to a stated
.I unavailable
line rather than a guessed number. Like the ccusage cross-check the comparison
is computed over the whole date-comparable scope, not a dimension-filtered
slice \(em the subscription fee is global, so comparing a per-repo estimate
against the whole plan fee would be apples-to-oranges. The
.I spend
schema and worked examples are in
.I instruments/README.md
in the atelier repository.
.SH ROLLUP LEDGER
A wide
.B \-\-from\-archive
run re-reads and re-decompresses every file in the mirror \(em cheap per file,
but the whole preserved history is roughly a gigabyte of gzip a year, so a
repeated historical query pays that cost every time. The archive already
guarantees the numbers survive pruning, so this is purely a
.I speed
concern, not a data one.
.B ccrepo
answers it with a machine-local rollup ledger,
.IR ~/.claude/ccrepo-rollup.json ,
that caches each source file's parsed, priced messages under a cheap validity
fingerprint (its modification time and size). Because the archive is
append-only, an existing file's fingerprint never changes \(em it is always a
cache hit \(em while a newly-archived session is a miss, read and priced once
and then folded in. A warm run decompresses only what is genuinely new.
.PP
The ledger is a pure speed layer: a cached message is grouped, filtered and
reconciled by exactly the same code an uncached one is, so it changes how fast a
total is produced, never the total itself. It is keyed per file rather than per
calendar period on purpose \(em a month cannot be validated without first
reading files to learn each message's timestamp, and a session straddling a
month boundary would be misfiled by any date proxy; file-grain keying sidesteps
both and leaves month, quarter or any grouping to be computed from the true
timestamps downstream, identical to an uncached run. Cost and the
plan-covered/uncovered split are stored per message, so they depend on the price
table and the billing covers-list; a change to either is detected by a recipe
signature and rebuilds the ledger automatically. It is used transparently
whenever it is present;
.B \-\-no\-rollup
bypasses it, and deleting the file forces a clean rebuild on the next run. A
corrupt or wrong-version ledger is rebuilt, never fatal. The ledger holds only
token counts and cost per message \(em never prompt or response text \(em but as
a derivative of a person's usage it stays machine-local and never enters a
repository.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/
The source: Claude Code's raw session logs, read read-only. A repo's true name
is recovered from the
.I cwd
recorded inside each log, not from the dash-mangled folder name.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Mobile\ Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/cc-transcripts/
The default
.BR ccarchive (1)
mirror
.B \-\-from\-archive
reads \(em the same layout, gzip-compressed. Overridden by
.B \-\-dest
or
.BR $CCARCHIVE_DEST .
.TP
.I ~/.claude/ccrepo-pricing.json
Optional local price overrides, merged over the built-in table. Malformed JSON
is ignored with a warning, never fatal.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/ccrepo-billing.json
Optional plan config that enables the
.I Actual
column and, via an optional
.I spend
block, the actual-spend-versus-estimate reconciliation. Malformed or missing a
positive
.I plan.monthlyCost
\(em ignored with a warning; a bad
.I spend.mode
falls back to plan mode and a malformed period entry is dropped, never fatal.
Holds a person's plan and real spend, so it stays machine-local and never enters
a repository.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/ccrepo-rollup.json
The rollup ledger \(em a machine-local speed cache of parsed, priced messages
for
.B \-\-from\-archive
runs, keyed per source file by modification time and size. Rebuilt automatically
when stale, corrupt or wrong-version; bypass with
.BR \-\-no\-rollup ,
delete to force a clean rebuild. Holds usage-derived figures, so it stays
machine-local and never enters a repository. See
.B ROLLUP LEDGER
above.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ccrepo
Per-repo totals in NZD (the default).
.TP
.B ccrepo \-g month
Totals per calendar month.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-g repo,model
Each repo, broken down by model.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-g model,repo
Each model, broken down by repo.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-g repo,branch \-\-repo ros
Just the
.I ros
repo, broken down by branch.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-g kind
Main-thread spend versus subagent spend.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-g repo \-\-sort name
Repos listed alphabetically instead of by cost.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-g repo,model \-\-sort "cost+name"
Repos by cost, models within each tied on cost broken alphabetically.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-g session \-\-context 500k\-
Sessions whose peak context window reached 500k tokens or more.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-g session \-\-sort cost \-\-top 20
The 20 costliest sessions, not several hundred.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-\-fx usd \-\-no\-reconcile
Raw list-price figures, offline, with no ccusage cross-check.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-\-from\-archive \-g month
Monthly totals over the whole preserved history, including pruned sessions. The
first such run builds the rollup ledger; later ones re-read only new files.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-\-from\-archive \-g month \-\-no\-rollup
The same, but bypassing the rollup ledger for a from-scratch re-walk.
.TP
.B ccrepo \-\-json > usage.json
Tidy JSON for scripting.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success.
.PP
.B 2
on a usage error \(em an unknown
.B \-g
dimension, an unknown or badly-directed
.B \-\-sort
key,
.B \-\-json
and
.B \-\-csv
together,
.B \-\-rate
on USD figures where it has no effect, or a non-positive
.BR \-\-rate .
.PP
.B 1
on a runtime failure \(em notably a currency conversion whose rate could not be
fetched (offline, with no
.B \-\-rate
override), or any unexpected error.
.SH NOTES
Cost is an API-equivalent estimate at list prices, not your subscription bill;
the
.I Actual
column reprices it under a plan, and the
.I spend
block (see
.BR BILLING )
reconciles it against what you genuinely pay. Neither is fetched from a billing
API \(em there is none \(em so both are only as true as the machine-local
config.
.PP
Calendar buckets (day, hour, week, and the
.BR \-\-since / \-\-until
filters) use
.I local
time on purpose \(em a day means the local one. Currency defaults to NZD and is
converted live, so a plain run needs network access both for the conversion and
for the
.BR ccusage (1)
cross-check;
.B \-\-fx usd
and
.B \-\-no\-reconcile
together make the run fully offline. No personal path is baked into the tool:
the source, price and billing locations are all derived from the home directory
at runtime.
